		<h2>3. How Time-of-Use (TOU) Arbitrage Works</h2>
		<p>A home energy battery operates under a simple financial mechanism known as TOU rate arbitrage:</p>

		<ul class="article-list">
			<li><strong>09:00 AM - 03:00 PM:</strong> Solar panels generate surplus energy. Rather than exporting to PG&E, SCE, or SDG&E for $0.05/kWh, power is routed into local battery storage.</li>
			<li><strong>04:00 PM - 09:00 PM:</strong> Grid rates rise to peak pricing ($0.52–$0.65/kWh). The battery system automatically discharges to cover household load, eliminating grid purchases at peak prices.</li>
		</ul>

		<h2>4. Federal Tax Credits & California Rebates</h2>
		<p>Residential battery systems installed alongside solar (or retrofit) qualify for the <strong>30% Residential Clean Energy Credit</strong> under Section 25D of the Internal Revenue Code. Additional local equity rebates through the Self-Generation Incentive Program (SGIP) may further offset battery equipment expenses for eligible utility customers.</p>

		<h2>5. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
		
		<h3>Is solar still worth it in California under NEM 3.0?</h3>
		<p>Yes, provided the system is sized appropriately and includes battery storage to maximize self-consumption rather than grid export.</p>

		<h3>Do existing NEM 1.0 or NEM 2.0 customers get affected?</h3>
		<p>No. Systems grandfathered under NEM 1.0 or NEM 2.0 retain their original tariff structure for 20 years from their original permission to operate (PTO) date.</p>

